# ---- ¶5 the dog
dict(mt="Xch'a k'u ajaw chike Chi pusu ri nu tz'i' chi k'astaj chi u wach iwumal xe uch'axik We xech'a",
 l="Said then the-lord to-them: “Sacrifice the my-dog; let-it-come-alive (chi) its-face by-you,” they-were-told. “Yes,” they-said.",
 i="Then the lord said to them: “Sacrifice my dog, and bring him back to life,” they were told. “Very well,” they said.",
 n="nu ꜩy 'my dog'. The manuscript writes ꜩy here and twice more, with a small mark over the y. It is the same word as the published ꜩi 'dog' (003, 004), and the toggle has the same form, tz'i'. It is printed as written. chi caztah … v vach 'let its face come alive', the K'iche' way of saying 'come back to life'. Here the word is written with plain c, and in the rest of the part it is written with the cuatrillo (ꜭaztah). Published parts have both spellings, and the toggle has k'astaj for both. chi before v vach is either chic 'again' with its c not written or chi 'at, before'. It is not clear which, and it is not rendered here (021 read it 'before their eyes (?)'). ivumal 'by you (pl.)'. Ve 'yes, very well', the we 'if' of earlier parts used here as an answer, as Brasseur takes it ('C'est bien')."),
dict(mt="Ta xki pus tz'i' xk'astai chi u wach",
 l="Then they-sacrificed the-dog, it-came-alive (chi) its-face;",
 i="So they sacrificed the dog, and it came back to life;",
 n="xqui puz 'they sacrificed it' (puz, as puzu). xꜭaztai has a doubtful ending. The last letter is an i with an accent stroke, not h, and Brasseur prints x-qaztal. The word is read as the xꜭaztah 'it came alive' of the next line and is respelled by the letters (xk'astai). The q-shaped letter before a is taken as the cuatrillo, as everywhere in this part."),
dict(mt="qitzij k'u chi kikot ri tz'i' ta xk'astaj u wach chu sakb'isala uje' ta xk'astaj u wach",
 l="truly then (chi) it-rejoiced the dog when it-came-alive its-face; it-wagged(?) its-tail when it-came-alive its-face.",
 i="truly the dog was glad when it came back to life, and it wagged its tail (?) when it came back to life.",
 n="quicot 'be glad, rejoice'. ta xꜭaztah v vach 'when it came back to life' is said twice, closing each half of the line. zacbizala is not met before; 'wag' follows Brasseur ('il remuait sa queue'). It may be built with -iza 'make' (inferred; toggle sakb'isala by the letters). chu 'it', ch- + v-, before the verb. vhe 'its tail' (modern uje')."),
